exp100: a certified-to-be filter of hard-to-round candidates of exp
=====================================================================

The search htr.c, run with a coarse window, prints many candidates x.
Each is then filtered at run time: maybe_hard(x) answers 0 only when x is
surely not hard, in the sense of code/exptablekl/ExpHard.v (with e the
binade of exp x, v = 2^(max(e, -1022) - 53), x is hard iff exp(x)/v is
within 2^-43 of an integer).  It may answer 1 for a non-hard x, never 0
for a hard one.

Files
-----
exp100.h       the interface and the parameters (NL, P, K, DEG, EXP100_D)
exp100.c       the evaluator and the filter, in plain C for VST
exp100_table.h generated: the constants T, C, LN2, RMAX, INV
gen_table.c    the generator (MPFR, not trusted); `gen_table budget`
               prints the error budget below
test_exp100.c  the tests, against MPFR at 300 bits
Makefile       make (tests built), make test, make budget

What it computes
----------------
exp_encl_bits(xb, M, s), xb the 64 bits of a double x with |x| < 1024
(zero and subnormal x accepted): returns 0 and M (3 words of 64 bits,
2^160 <= M < 2^162) and s with

    | exp(x) - M 2^s | <= D 2^s,   D = EXP100_D = 16,

so the relative error is below 2^-156.  It returns a nonzero code for
|x| >= 1024, infinities and NaN, and when a run-time check of the
reduction fails (never in the tests).  exp_encl(double) only reads the
bits of x (a union) and calls it; this wrapper is outside the proof.

maybe_hard_bits(xb): runs exp_core, then
  - 1 if y - D and y + D (y = M) have different numbers of bits: the
    enclosure does not fix the binade e;
  - with e = s + bitlen(y) - 1 and f = max(e, -1022) - 53 - s,
    exp(x)/v = y / 2^f; 1 if f is outside [64, 184] (cannot happen on
    -745.14 < x < 709.79, where f is in [107, 163]);
  - d = the distance of y to the nearest multiple of 2^f; 0 if
    d > 2^(f-43) + D, else 1.
Soundness of the 0: every z in [y - D, y + D] is at distance at least
d - D > 2^(f-43) from the multiples of 2^f, so exp(x)/v = z/2^f is at
distance more than 2^-43 from every integer.  Subnormal results
(e < -1022, v = 2^-1075) are handled by the same formula, no special case.

Design choices (choices, not constraints)
-----------------------------------------
- Numbers: NL = 6 limbs of 32 bits (192 bits), each limb in a uint64_t,
  fixed point with P = 160 bits after the point.  Limbs of 32 bits so that
  every step a_i b_j + p + c of a product fits a 64-bit word: no 128-bit
  type (CompCert has none) and no carry-by-comparison.  The output M is
  repacked into 3 words of 64 bits.
- K = 6: a table of 64 values 2^(j/64); small, to certify in Rocq.
- DEG = 16: the Taylor remainder is below 2 u (u = 2^-160).
- Reduction: n = floor(|x| 64/ln2) is guessed with one 32-bit constant
  INV, corrected once in each direction, then checked:
  q(n) <= X < q(n+1) with q(n) = floor(n LN2 / 2^32), LN2 = ln2/64 2^192.
  For x >= 0, N = n and r = X - q(n); for x < 0, N = -(n+1) and
  r = q(n+1) - X.  So r >= 0 always (no signed numbers anywhere); r is
  checked below RMAX = ceil(ln2/64 2^160) + 3.
- N = 64 h + j is computed as N + 2^17 (unsigned), j its 6 low bits;
  s = h - 160.
- The precision, 156 bits, is far more than the 97 bits the decision needs
  (2^-43 on a value of 2^54); it costs one limb more than 128 bits and
  keeps every error term at a few units of 2^-160.

Error budget (`make budget`, computed by MPFR, rounded up; u = 2^-160)
--------------------------------------------------------------------
With r* = x - N ln2/64 the exact reduced argument, r the computed one
(r <= rmax = ln2/64 + 3u < 0.010831), h the Horner value, p the Taylor
polynomial of degree 16, and exp(x) = 2^h 2^(j/64) exp(r*):

  source                                       bound
  reduced argument |r - r*|                    2.0000 u  (x truncated to
                                                P bits: 1; floor of n LN2:
                                                1; LN2 to nearest, n < 2^17:
                                                2^-16)
  Horner truncations 0 <= p(r) - h u           2.0219 u  (1 floor and the
                                                rounding down of 1/i!, per
                                                step: e_i = e_(i+1) r + 2)
  Taylor remainder 0 <= exp(r) - p(r)          1.6122 u  (rmax^17/17! e^rmax)
  table |T_j u - 2^(j/64)|                     0.5 u     (to nearest)

  contributions to |y u - 2^(j/64) exp(r*)|:
    reduced argument   2 e^rmax (e^(2.0000 u) - 1)   4.0436 u
    table              e^rmax / 2                    0.5054 u
    Horner and Taylor  2 (2.0219 + 1.6122)           7.2681 u
    last product       floor                         1.0000 u
    total                                           12.8171 u  = 2^-156.32

D = 16 covers the total (y >= 2^160, so the bound is also relative).

Tests (measured, `make test` and the candidate files; one process, pinned
with taskset -c 2 on the desktop, turbo off)
---------------------------------------------------------------------
  input set                      n     rejected  outside  max error
  random (3 kinds, see below)    100000   0         0       7.39 u
  boundaries                     65116    0         0       8.05 u
  in-1074 line 1, MAXN 10^6      3        0         0       1.12 u
  in1-52 line 1, MAXN 10^5       1536     0         0       0.33 u
  (max error: |MPFR exp(x) - M 2^s| / 2^s, against the bound D = 16)

  decision          MPFR hard  maybe_hard=1  unsound  clear non-hard -> 1
  random            29876      29876         0        0
  boundaries        79         79            0        0
  in-1074           1          1             0        0
  in1-52            512        512           0        0
No input was within 2^-100 of the threshold 2^-43 (all decisions clear);
on every input maybe_hard agrees with MPFR.  The 7 rejected inputs
(|x| >= 1024, infinities, NaN) are rejected.

Random: a third uniform in value on (-745.14, 709.79), a third with a
uniform biased exponent over every normal binade up to 2^9, a third with
|x| in [2^-60, 2^10).  The hard ones are the tiny x of the second third
(|x| < about 2^-96: exp(x)/v is then within 2^-43 of 2^53 or 2^54).
Boundaries: 17 doubles around each of -745.14, -745.13, the x with exp(x)
near 2^-1075 and 2^-1022, 709.78, log(DBL_MAX), 709.79, +-2^-53, +-2^-54,
+-1, +-2^-1022, +-2^-60, and around the double nearest k ln2/64 for k = -99
.. 99 and every 37th k from -68800 to 65500; plus 0, -0, +-2^-1074,
+-the largest subnormal.
Candidates: `cd ../capla/htr3/run && OMP_NUM_THREADS=1 make in-1074.htr
RANGE=1-1 MAXN=1000000 TABLES=/home/thery/claudeExp/paul-klvar` (and
in1-52.htr RANGE=1-1 MAXN=100000), then `./test_exp100
../capla/htr3/run/in-1074.htr ../capla/htr3/run/in1-52.htr`.

Time per call (measured, same run): exp_encl 1.63 us, maybe_hard 2.1 us.
The whole `make test` takes 2.2 s.

clightgen -normalize (switch vst, CompCert 3.17) accepts exp100.c.

Proof plan
----------
The goal: for every xb, maybe_hard_bits(xb) = 0 implies not (hard x),
x the double of bits xb.  Each piece is a file with a fixed statement.

1. Word arithmetic (VST, like code/APaul/vst/Verif_add.v).  One
   semax_body per function of exp100.c, on lists of limbs < 2^32 and
   valZ = sum a_i 2^(32 i): num_zero, num_copy, num_add (a + b, given it
   fits), num_sub (a - b, given a >= b), num_lt, num_mul_small (a w),
   num_mulshr (floor(a b / 2^160), given it fits), num_bitlen, num_pow2,
   num_low (a mod 2^f), num_scale (floor(v 2^e)), mul_ln2, guess_n.
   Every loop is a counted for loop; the only overflow facts are
   (2^32-1)^2 + 2 (2^32-1) = 2^64 - 1 and the stated size bounds.
2. exp_core in integers (VST).  Its postcondition is an integer
   statement: with X = floor(|x| 2^160) (x read from its bits, as Flocq's
   B2R of the bits), q(n) <= X < q(n+1); r as defined; r < RMAX;
   h = Horner(C, r) with floors; y = floor(T_j h / 2^160); N, j, h as
   defined.  No real numbers in this step.
3. The constants (Rocq, Interval by reflection, or exact rationals): the
   T_j within 1/2 of 2^(j/64) 2^160 (64 goals), LN2 within 1/2 of
   ln2/64 2^192, RMAX = ceil(ln2/64 2^160) + 3 (so r < RMAX gives
   r u <= rmax), C_i = floor(2^160 / i!) (exact, by computation).  INV
   needs no certificate: n is checked.  PROVED in rocq/ (ExpConsts.v:
   T_ok, LN2_ok, RMAX_ok, ltZ_rmax, C_ok, and the Taylor line of the
   budget, taylor_rem_le); ExpTable.v is generated from exp100_table.h
   by gen_v.py and make checks that they agree.
4. The error bound (Rocq, reals, no C): from step 2's integer relations
   and step 3's bounds, |exp(x) - y 2^(h-160)| <= 16 2^(h-160).  The
   pieces are those of the budget table: the Taylor remainder (Rocq's
   Taylor lemmas or Interval on r <= rmax), the Horner induction
   e_i <= e_(i+1) r + 2, the reduced argument, the table, the last floor;
   each is one lemma, summed in a final one.  The budget leaves 3.18 u of
   slack.
5. The decision (Rocq, reals): with the enclosure of step 4, the integer
   tests of maybe_hard_bits (equal bit lengths of y - D and y + D; d >
   2^(f-43) + D) imply the binade e = s + bitlen(y) - 1 and
   |exp(x)/v - z| >= 2^-43 for every integer z, that is not (hard x) with
   ExpHard.v's definitions.
6. Assembly: semax_func over all functions of exp100.c (as Verif_htr3.v),
   then the theorem above.  Not proved: the union wrappers exp_encl and
   maybe_hard (they only read the bits), the generator, the tests.
